From: Łukasz Buśko <lukasz.busko@guns4hire.cc>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:27:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: mxl862xx: support passive SerDes mux

The 10G LAN SerDes of the Bananapi BPi-R4 Pro 8X reaches either the
external Aeonsemi AS21010 PHY or the SFP1 cage through a passive mux
driven by the SFP1 module detect line. Describe that mux below the
switch node and move the DSA port between its two channels at runtime:

  ds_mux: ds-mux-bus {
      ds_mux0: ds-mux@13 {
          compatible = "mxl862xx,ds-mux";
          reg = <13>;
          chan-sel-gpios = <&pio 54 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
          sfp-present-channel = <0>;
          channel@0 { reg = <0>; phy-mode = "10gbase-r";
                      managed = "in-band-status"; sfp = <&sfp1>; };
          channel@1 { reg = <1>; phy-mode = "usxgmii";
                      managed = "in-band-status";
                      phy-handle = <&phy24>; };
      };
  };

The port node itself describes the PHY lane, so the mux starts on it and
a delayed work switches to the SFP lane while a module sits in the cage.
That presence is not polled on a second copy of the module detect GPIO,
which would need the deprecated GPIOD_FLAGS_BIT_NONEXCLUSIVE: the mux
resolves the cage referenced by its SFP channel with
sfp_bus_find_fwnode() and asks it through
sfp_bus_module_present(), which has to be called under the rtnl lock.

Kernel 6.18 requires the phylink of a channel to be created on demand
and destroyed when leaving it: a phylink owns the SerDes PCS returned by
mac_select_pcs(), so both channels may not hold one at the same time.
phylink_create() and phylink_destroy() also take the rtnl lock while
registering and unregistering the sfp bus, while phylink_stop(),
phylink_start() and phylink_disconnect_phy() assert it. The swap
therefore runs in three steps: stop the link and disconnect the phy
under rtnl, create the phylink of the new channel without that lock,
then install it, select the channel and reconnect under rtnl again. The
phylink of the previous channel is destroyed last, outside rtnl, once
the new instance has taken the PCS objects. dp->dn is swapped together
with dp->pl and the port node reference is restored when the mux is
released, leaving DSA owning exactly one reference either way.

A channel whose phylink cannot be created is not installed: the phy of
the channel the port was already on is reconnected and its link
restarted, so a failed switch leaves the port running instead of down
until a later poll succeeds.

The channel select line is driven with the channel the port node
describes, so the hardware and the driver agree before the first poll. A
poll that finds the cage of the SFP channel not bound yet leaves the
lane alone and only reschedules the work, instead of recording a channel
it never selected.

Muxes are added after dsa_register_switch() because the phylink of a
user port only exists once its netdev has been created, and are
released before dsa_unregister_switch() and dsa_switch_shutdown() so
that DSA always destroys the phylink currently installed on the port.

Based on the BPi-R4 Pro 8X board support from the Bananapi vendor tree.
